setup:
Create a directional field with one central and two outside exit gates.
Mark a scoring zone at each end.
Divide players into an exit team and a pressing team.
Before each possession, the pressing team receives a structure card naming one of several presses:
corridor lock;
boundary trap;
high man-on-man press;
delayed zone press.
The exit team is not told which structure will be used.
The press may remain disguised until the first disposal.

Create a directional field with a scoring zone at each end.
Divide players into two teams.
Prepare scoreboard scenarios such as:
leading by one point with ninety seconds remaining;
trailing by four points with one minute remaining;
scores level with thirty seconds remaining;
leading by seven points with two minutes remaining.
Display the score and remaining time.
Use scoring values that allow realistic scenario outcomes.

Create a directional field with a scoring zone at each end.
Divide players into two teams.
Nominate two or three player leaders for each team.
Give each team an initial tactical objective.
The coach may privately give one team a tactical advantage, such as:
an extra player behind the football;
a boundary trap;
a corridor lock;
a high defensive press.
The opposition must identify and solve the problem during play.

Create a directional field with a scoring zone at each end.
Divide players into two teams.
The coach or a qualified assistant acts as umpire.
Prepare a range of match events:
advantage;
free-kick reversal;
fifty-metre penalty simulation;
boundary throw-in;
ball-up;
play on;
touched disposal;
score review simulation;
incorrect or delayed player assumption.
Explain that all decisions are training constraints and are not open to argument.

Create a reduced full-ground playing area.
Divide players into two balanced teams.
Nominate a captain, defensive leader, midfield leader and forward leader for each team.
Give teams five minutes to establish:
starting structure;
defensive priority;
ball-movement plan;
stoppage roles;
interchange plan;
scoreboard-management principles.
The coach establishes safety rules, playing time and scoring only.
During play, the coach does not provide tactical instructions.

Create a reduced match-simulation field appropriate to the age and ability of the group.
Mark a scoring zone at each end.
Add optional side scoring gates, lanes and field thirds.
Divide players into two balanced teams.
Nominate player leaders.
Prepare assessment categories covering:
possession;
width and depth;
support;
movement after disposal;
scoring-zone use;
turnover reaction;
contested possession;
pressure;
defensive shape;
team ball movement;
tactical adaptability;
scoreboard management;
communication;
leadership.
Conduct five progressive phases.
Contact, field size and workload must be modified for younger, returning and Over 35 players.
